---
import Shell from "../components/Shell.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<Shell
  title="Terms | Triad"
  description="The rules for using Triad as an identity broker and OAuth authorization service."
  mode="product"
>
  <article class="legal-page shell" aria-labelledby="terms-title">
    <header class="legal-heading">
      <p>TERMS / IDENTITY BROKER</p>
      <h1 id="terms-title">USE THE<br /><span>CONTRACT.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Triad lets a provider-authenticated person approve narrow identity claims for a registered application.
        These terms describe the boundaries of that service.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="legal-section" aria-labelledby="terms-service">
      <p class="section-kicker">01 / SERVICE</p>
      <h2 id="terms-service">WHAT TRIAD DOES</h2>
      <p>
        Triad provides provider sign-in, account-scoped identity values, consent screens, and OAuth-compatible
        authorization flows. Device approval can authorize a Triad session or a registered OAuth client. An
        application receives only the scopes and resource permissions you approve, subject to the protocol
        response and the application’s own terms.
      </p>
      <p>
        Triad's application records do not retain request IP addresses, authentication paths, or browser user-agent
        strings. Session records keep only the data needed to authenticate the session. Rate-limit records keep an
        opaque keyed bucket, a request count, and a timestamp.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-section" aria-labelledby="terms-responsibilities">
      <p class="section-kicker">02 / RESPONSIBILITIES</p>
      <h2 id="terms-responsibilities">KEEP ACCESS UNDER CONTROL</h2>
      <p>Use an upstream provider account you are authorized to use. Match every device code exactly. Review the Client ID, token result, requested scopes, Protected Resource, Wallet Namespace, Wallet Profile, Account Index, resolved Derivation Path, applicable Chain ID, and Signing Envelope before approval. Keep your provider, Passkeys, and browser access secure, and report a suspected unauthorized connection through the project’s source repository.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-section" aria-labelledby="terms-connections">
      <p class="section-kicker">03 / CONNECTIONS</p>
      <h2 id="terms-connections">APPLICATIONS STAND APART</h2>
      <p>
        A downstream application is responsible for its own service, security, availability, and use of the claims
        it receives. Triad does not endorse an application merely because it appears in an authorization request.
        Removing consent affects stored approval; a token that has already been issued may remain valid until it
        expires.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-section" aria-labelledby="terms-boundaries">
      <p class="section-kicker">04 / BOUNDARIES</p>
      <h2 id="terms-boundaries">NO MISUSE</h2>
      <p>
        Do not probe, overload, bypass safeguards, impersonate another person, submit misleading provider data, or
        use Triad to violate law or another service’s terms. Triad may suspend or change a flow when necessary to
        protect the service, its users, or an upstream provider.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-section" aria-labelledby="terms-changes">
      <p class="section-kicker">05 / CHANGES</p>
      <h2 id="terms-changes">THE SERVICE ITERATES</h2>
      <p>
        Triad is an evolving project. The service, supported providers, protocol behavior, and these terms may
        change as the implementation changes. Continued use after a published update means you accept the current
        version. If you do not accept a change, stop using the service and delete the account.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Shell>
